# Audit reported map-wide LOD/geometry overlaps before removing any vertices;…

Audit reported map-wide LOD/geometry overlaps before removing any vertices;
permit only deterministic source-backed conversion corrections.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Audit reported map-wide LOD/geometry overlaps before removing any
  vertices; permit only deterministic source-backed conversion corrections.

## Dependencies

No explicit task dependency was recorded in the legacy TODO.

## Evidence

The current world conversion has no map-wide overlap-removal pass. Normal
world meshes use the strict authored-topology loader, and its retained
`discarded_degenerate_triangles` compatibility count is always zero. Layout
validation explicitly permits source overlap and rejects only malformed or
non-finite bounds rather than deleting geometry by distance, extent, or LOD.

The previous fused-interior path attempted exact face ownership using mesh and
primitive-group identity, shader, positions, UVs, normals, colors, and winding.
That policy could not establish cross-package ownership: every production mesh
name already embeds both package id and member id before fusion, so equivalent
faces from different level variants can never share its fingerprint. The only
possible removals were repeated value-equivalent faces inside one authored
mesh/group.

A complete audit of all 19 interior packages covers 783 direct render meshes and
168,653 decoded triangle records. The old fingerprint removed exactly two
records, both from Level 7 Simpsons House mesh `int_sh_wallsShape_001`, group 0,
shader `int_sh_wall_m`. The authored pairs are `(2, 3, 13)` versus `(13, 3, 1)`
and `(100, 40, 102)` versus `(102, 40, 101)`: distinct vertex indices resolve to
identical corner values, so value-based ownership collapsed their second
occurrence even though both records are serialized by the source.

Shipped `tPrimGroupLoader::LoadStreamed` copies every `INDEXLIST` entry into the
primitive group, and streamed drawing submits the complete `mIndexCount`; it has
no face-deduplication step. Interior fusion therefore now appends source meshes
in authored order without geometry suppression. The compatibility
`removed_duplicate_triangles` field remains published as zero, and a focused
regression proves value-equivalent authored face occurrences survive fusion.
Visual overlap alone remains insufficient evidence for future removal.

A fresh 129-package post-fix replay publishes the same 4,002 files and 12,240
source placements with all eight interior removal counts at zero. Its output is
449,794,288 bytes versus 449,794,224 before the fix, a bounded 64-byte increase
while package, placement, and excluded-collision counts remain unchanged.

The duplicate-shader publication blocker is now resolved from shipped Pure3D
inventory semantics: same-section shader collisions are first-store-wins, and
the terrain-world ledger proves that authoritative first occurrences precede
their consumers. A fresh 129-package replay then exposed an unrelated
`shrubRound.png` scratch-name collision between independent shader resolutions.
Per-shader scratch isolation preserves both differing payloads and advances the
replay beyond that staging-only blocker.

Across all 129 selected terrain-world packages, 9,765 decoded meshes contain
2,163 triangle-list primitive groups. Twelve groups contain 28 repeated-index
triangle records, including `[786, 782, 782]` and `[794, 794, 785]` in
`common_gens203Shape_054`. The source-preserving analysis path now retains all
such authored records while ordinary FBX mesh loading remains strict.

Shipped Pure3D independently requires that choice for render and intersect data.
`tPrimGroupLoader::LoadStreamed` stores serialized render indices unchanged,
while `IntersectLoader` and `IntersectDSG::mTri` preserve exact intersect
triples.
The replay now advances through both topology boundaries without counting those
records as discarded.

A live Unreal Engine 5.8.1 MCP import audit now proves that exact FBX transport
does not make those records safe StaticMesh input. A synthetic FBX containing
only `[0, 0, 1]` is parsed successfully but rejected because all polygons are
degenerate. A second FBX containing one valid triangle plus one repeated-index
triangle reaches a fatal error inside the FBX SDK smoothing conversion before
any asset is created.

World FBX publication therefore now rejects repeated-index triangles before
writing an Unreal-bound artifact while decoded analysis keeps the exact authored
records. The task remains open until a target representation preserves that
source evidence without feeding unsafe geometry to Unreal.

The following material boundary is six package-local `lambert1` misses,
including `l3z3` mesh `z3_gens108Shape_071`. Shipped `tPrimGroupLoader` creates
`TShader("error")` when its temporary store and runtime inventory cannot resolve
the authored shader name. The migration path now reproduces that deterministic
fallback only for exact phase-three mesh consumers whose package-local shader is
proven absent; generic and unproven callers still fail closed. The map-wide
overlap audit remains open for any later screenshot-reported overlaps because
no remaining source-backed geometry-removal rule has been established.
